ake is the one is going to  get me fired and where am i going to be  then but though he was struggling conway  felt he could not ask for help going to  the chief of staff at the time wasn't an  option because  in my view that was putting my job in my  career on the line  now though as the new parliament gets  underway a group of parliamentarians is  urging colleagues to pay more attention  to their own mental health challenges  and to the mental health of their staff  it's not an easy job  it is not a nine to five job it is a  difficult difficult task awareness is  good  but it's not enough nova scotia senator  stan kutcher and toronto mp aris sachs  supervised the production of a mental  health handbook for parliamentarians  it's a resource guide to help mps  senators and their staffs we want to  make sure that in this unique  environment that we're working in that  our staff and ourselves have the  resources and tools we need to be able  to make good decisions  conway says this focus on mental health  is long overdue  when people are exhausted mentally  that is not the good conditions  the proper conditions to have great  policy decisions  which means improving mental health  among those working on parliament hill  could have a payoff for the entire  country  david aiken global news ottawa  still ahead the excruciating effects of  endometriosis and why it takes so long  to diagnose  endometriosis is an extremely painful  gynological disorder affecting more than  half a million women in canada but  experts believe that number is much  larger thousands of patients are being  misdiagnosed and even worse ignored as  jamie morocco reports doctors at one  toronto hospital are sounding the alarm  on what they call a substandard quality  of care  denise natalini says she suffered  excruciating period pains for more than  two decades it's to the point where i  was on the couch in the fetal position  sweating but doctor after doctor brushed  her symptoms off my quality of life  was terrible to the point where i no  longer could teach it turns out that now  50 year old had endometriosis a  gynecological condition where  endometrial tissue grows outside the  uterus causing pelvic pain soreness  during sex heavy menstrual bleeding  cramping and even infertility it's  estimated one in 10 women suffer from  endometriosis but in canada it takes  upwards of five years on average to get  a diagnosis toronto gynecologist dr  nacelio lemos who finally treated  natalini says he has a 500 patient wait  list alone  time between referral and surgery with  me today is at three years  because we don't have simply don't have  enough people lemos was recruited from  brazil in 2017. he says canada is years  behind in terms of testing and is still  using an invasive abdominal surgery  rather than the new standard an mri i  was shocked  to hear the term diagnostic laparoscopy  which i hadn't heard  for at least 15 years in brazil experts  say the fix is government funding health  canada says the onus is on provinces and  territories the funding is lacking the  understanding is lacking the advocacy is  lacking  and really the result is  is substandard care leaving more than an  estimated one million women like  natalini to advocate for themselves they  just didn't listen to me and  it's not normal to have this kind of  pain or risk suffering for years  without ever knowing why jamie morocco  global news toronto  up next a bc doctor's diagnosis of  climate change as a medical issue  there's a big focus on how climate  change is impacting the world around us  what may not be as evident is how it's  impacting our health a doctor in british  columbia is now giving some of his  patients a new diagnosis for what's  making them sick ted chernecki explains  this isn't the kootenais a family doctor  remembers as a boy i grew up in this  area and i don't remember  problems with heat or wildfire smoke  growing up at all  since i've been back for the past eight  years in the kootenays  more often than not during the summer  we're having a significant period of  wildfire smoke and then this past summer  with the heat tone  he and many of his colleagues in nelson  and elsewhere in the province have taken  to adding the words climate change when  filling out medical reports  no one can say for sure but logic  dictates that some of the patients  they're seeing shouldn't have symptoms  so severe if it wasn't for heat and  smoke and especially if it doesn't cool  off at night which it didn't this past  summer you know you get dehydrated that  makes it harder to manage your blood  sugars then your diabetes can go to  whack which makes it even harder and  even more likely to get dehydrated and  then you show up in emergency department  this map from health canada shows the  premature deaths per 100 000 for those  exposed to certain air pollutants worst  in the country is bc southern interior  and five of the top 15 regions in canada  are in bc with the okanagan similan  easily topping that list  an expert on air pollution believes our  health care system needs to be better  prepared for not if but when it's pretty  well understood that this is  something we should anticipate and it's  almost like we'll be lucky  if next summer we don't have something  like this he runs ubc's air pollution  exposure lab aimed at collecting the  science needed to know what air  pollutants via diesel fumes or smoke  particulates are doing to the human body  in some cases reducing pollution levels  can solve a lot of problems but that's  never going to happen with wildfires  people that can't afford air  conditioners etc that don't have them  they really should be supported by the  government because otherwise we're just  letting everyone down and we're not  talking about minor issues we're talking  about deaths nearly a thousand last  summer from heat alone so for the  beleaguered taxpayer subsidizing an air  purifier or an air conditioner for those  who can't afford it might be far cheaper  than even a short stay in hospital ted  chernake global news vancouver  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm robin gill tonight your  canada is this rainbow over marchmont  ontario we'd love to see your corner of  the country so please keep sending your  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  thank you for watching donna frieza will  be back at the anchor desk tomorrow and  i'll see you next weekend have a great  night  [Music]
 

